Title:
Signed to Kill: Reverse Engineering a 0-Day Used to Disable CrowdStrike EDR

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This write-up reverse engineers a Microsoft-signed Windows kernel driver abused in a BYOVD (Bring Your Own Vulnerable Driver) attack to terminate security/EDR processes.  
- The driver exposes an unsafe IOCTL interface that accepts a target PID (as an ASCII string) and kills the process from kernel context, enabling termination of protected EDR services like CrowdStrike Falcon.  
- The author analyzes the driver in IDA Pro, focuses on the IRP_MJ_DEVICE_CONTROL dispatch path, and identifies the “process killer” IOCTL (0x22E010) and the device symbolic link needed for user-mode access.  
- Internally, the driver uses ZwOpenProcess followed by ZwTerminateProcess, bypassing user-mode access controls that normally prevent killing PPL-protected processes.  
- A simple PoC is shown that opens the device via CreateFileW and sends the PID via DeviceIoControl to trigger termination.  
- It’s useful for red teams and malware developers studying BYOVD-based EDR tampering, and for defenders building detections around suspicious driver loads and IOCTL usage.

Technical Focus:
- BYOVD via Microsoft-signed vulnerable drivers
- Windows kernel driver reverse engineering (IDA Pro, dispatch routines)
- IOCTL interface abuse (IRP_MJ_DEVICE_CONTROL)
- PPL/EDR process termination from kernel mode
- ZwOpenProcess / ZwTerminateProcess process-kill primitives
- Device object / symbolic link discovery (WinObj)

Use Cases:
- Disable EDR/self-protection by killing agent processes using a trusted signed driver
- Build PoCs to validate BYOVD exposure and driver-blocking controls
- Develop detections for suspicious kernel driver loads and anomalous IOCTL patterns
- Threat hunting for known device names/symlinks and process-kill IOCTL activity
- Driver allowlist/blocklist testing (WDAC, vulnerable driver blocklist coverage)

Keywords:
BYOVD, Microsoft-signed driver, vulnerable driver, kernel-mode, Windows driver, IOCTL, IRP_MJ_DEVICE_CONTROL, DeviceIoControl, CreateFileW, symbolic link, device object, IDA Pro, reverse engineering, process termination, ZwOpenProcess, ZwTerminateProcess, PPL, CrowdStrike Falcon, EDR bypass, OSRLoader, sc.exe, WDAC, vulnerable driver blocklist